+<a id="edit-api-runtime-server"></a>
+### Runtime as its own server
+
+The server is an ordinary LMX application over the runtime that it serves. Its state, service bodies, model loop, catalogue query logic and local agents are expressed in LMX. Existing kernel receivers and narrow platform I/O bindings supply graph operations, invocation, storage and transport. The server does not introduce another evaluator, authoritative AST, graph serializer, agent scheduler or global object registry.
+
+Three entry paths share ordinary preparation, admission and owner-side invocation:
+
+| Entry | Input | Boundary |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Native LMX | One complete LMX document | A framed text transport or an ordinary local text call |
+| Local LMX | Actual typed values and prepared references | Ordinary call or Message/post to a stable service entry |
+| MCP | Standard JSON-RPC messages of an explicitly selected MCP version | A wire binding maps the admitted request to the same service calls |
+
+The native document is a program in LMX. Tables and hierarchical values retain their ordinary meaning. A local ready graph is not serialized and reparsed merely to use the service. Transport correlation IDs, SQL row keys and MCP resource URIs are not runtime object identities; a live operation still receives an ordinarily resolved real reference or PLACE in its permitted context.
+
+<a id="edit-api-shared-codec"></a>
+### One parser and the kernel codecs
+
+`sys\fromLmx(text)` produces a **clean graph suitable for compilation**. The same ordinary graph can be inspected, composed, edited or interpreted without first compiling it. A compile-ready representation does not imply that every future call is already admitted or that computed defaults and arbitrary tests have executed. Explicit `sys\compile` remains separate.
+
+`sys\toLmx(value)` produces LMX code from **any Structure and any L3Thread**, according to the complete [source codec contract](#source-codec). A Thread export includes the arena tables required by that contract. The live graph and this kernel receiver are the basis for inspection and export; a disk source file is optional provenance or a saved export. The server calls these kernel receivers and does not maintain its own serializer.
+
+There is one main LMX parser containing the square-bracket subparser. INDEX forms such as `var[arg:arg2:arg3]` use that component. In a position permitting a square operation, compact `[op` without a space after `[` selects it; spaced groups, TYPE prefixes and VALUE constructors retain their specified contexts. Once the subparser recognizes the start of an expression, it calls the main parser. It also delegates `{...}` to the main parser: it neither interprets that content itself nor searches ahead for the closing brace or the end of a delegated expression. It resumes at the exact returned position. All consumers then use **one shared grouping implementation**. Duplicate parsers, binding algorithms, precedence tables and adapter workarounds must be removed. Framing and JSON decoding do not perform this language work.
+
+<a id="edit-api-service-contract"></a>
+### Concrete service contract
+
+The [LMX source package](../runtime_server/README.md) supplies ordinary models and service bodies; required integrations are explicit typed slots. Its [API](../runtime_server/docs/API.ru.md) distinguishes contracts from implementations.
+
+| Entry | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| `service\submitText(source)` | Prepare the whole document through kernel `fromLmx` and the normal language path; execute on the service owner |
+| `service\submitGraph(graph)` | Admit and execute a ready graph without a text roundtrip |
+| `service\replaceHandler(replacement)` | Admit and write the real callable slot on the same owner |
+| `runtime\inspect(scope; target; projection)` | Return the requested actual observation through the common graph/codec path |
+| `runtime\insert(scope; target; source)` | Insert exact source with ordinary owner-side placement; do not invoke a newly declared body |
+| `runtime\copyTo(scope; target; source)` | Copy one prepared source and explicitly place it through common copy/lifetime rules |
+| `runtime\step(scope; execution; action)` | Advance the specified execution to a supported observation point |
+| `model\complete(exchange)` | Return a whole model answer or the actual incomplete/failure outcome |
+
+`LmxServerStage` carries `ready` and `payload`: either the prepared invocation or the real diagnostic receipt. `LmxServerReceipt` carries `ok` and `payload`: a real result, an explicit ordinary void result, supported execution state or actual diagnostics. Neither an uninitialized reference nor numeric zero substitutes for a missing result. These are application models, not kernel categories.
+
+<a id="edit-api-agent-loop"></a>
+### User request and model loop
+
+The user invokes an ordinary LMX agent method. The agent calls its explicit model provider, receives a complete LMX document, submits it to the same runtime service, and supplies the actual receipt to the next model round when needed. The provider does not execute commands itself.
+
+The source package defines an optional agent-result profile: the executed program returns an ordinary `LmxServerDecision` with `finished` and `observation`. The agent admits this structural requirement before reading the fields. A generic native command or MCP tool is not forced to return that application-specific type. The agent has a finite round budget and uses existing Message/post callbacks and waits. Exhausted budget, incomplete text, unsupported result, uncertain delivery and runtime failure remain distinct outcomes. Late answers retain the ordinary Message contract. Completion depends on the actual result, not the model's claim.
+
+<a id="edit-api-live-handler"></a>
+### Behavior changes without recompilation
+
+A stable service entry reads a **real mutable callable reference** on its owner's lane at each new invocation. `replaceHandler` admits the replacement and writes the real slot through ordinary graph assignment. A new callable without native association follows the interpreter branch of the common dispatcher. Its actual DATA, parent and CODE remain intact. Adding and selecting this interpreted behavior needs no compilation.
+
+Already selected callables, formed Message calls and active frames retain their chosen reference and normal lifetime. A sender wanting the behavior current at service time posts to the stable entry, which reads the slot after delivery; a Message to an earlier resolved handler is not retargeted. Replacement does not rewrite direct native A→B calls, clear native descriptors, force walker execution, track graph writes or retry failed native work. Explicit compilation and a successful `endturn` remain the separate native-update contract.
+
+<a id="edit-api-catalogue"></a>
+### Compilation catalogue and live source
+
+Compilation may catalogue any useful facts it actually has about the supplied clean graph. The initial storage profile is one principal SQLite facts table per L3Thread with indexes and query projections; provider code owns table namespaces. This is a searchable projection of a stated compilation scope, not another authoritative program representation or Thread membership registry.
+
+Each result identifies its compilation/snapshot basis, owner partition, scope, coverage and evidence. Snapshot-local keys preserve links; duplicate field names and unnamed occurrences are not merged. Such a key is never an unrestricted runtime address. A current operation still uses contextual resolution or a retained actual reference and normal admission.
+
+<a id="edit-api-catalogue-facts"></a>
+### Useful catalogue facts
+
+| Category | Useful information | Precision boundary |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Graph inventory | Nodes, typed values, ordered field occurrences, empty/uninitialized slots, links, sharing, cycles, actual parents | Preserve snapshot identity; names alone are not keys |
+| Call contracts | Written/normalized types, roles, signatures, named parameters, required/default/absence, result, declared throws | Do not execute a deferred initializer for indexing |
+| Definitions and uses | Declarations, occurrences, lexical context, bindings and paths | Identify the preparation for which resolution is known |
+| Calls and effects | Written calls, resolved targets, candidates or unknown heads, potential reads/writes, explicit throw sites | Possible is not executed; no found edge is not proof of purity |
+| Dependencies | Import/copy sources, type relations, static caller/callee and native units | Support explicit impact/compilation planning, not automatic invalidation |
+| Native association | Actual module/entry/ABI and available artifact evidence | No portable machine-address identity or promise of matching later edits |
+| Documentation | Author comments, descriptions, examples, provenance, optional file/span/hash | Runtime-created graphs need no fictitious disk file |
+| Tests and observations | Declared tests separately from actual run results and diagnostics with their basis | A test declaration or successful build is not a passed-test receipt |
+| Coverage | Traversed scope, analyses performed, unresolved and unsupported facts | Full topology and full effect knowledge are different claims |
+
+Evidence distinguishes `written`, `graph-observed`, `resolved-for-this-compilation`, `runtime-observed`, `dynamic-unresolved` and `not-analysed`. Derived analysis preserves its basis and does not replace author data. SQL does not become another binding engine, overload selector, test-discovery mechanism or dependency scheduler.
+
+The [SQLite design and executable fixtures](../runtime_server/docs/CATALOGUE_SQLITE.ru.md) specify storage, queries and publication validation. Answers may be ordinary LMX tables or hierarchical structures. Full `payload_lmx` comes from kernel `toLmx`; a search snippet is explicitly not a complete export.
+
+<a id="edit-api-catalogue-publication"></a>
+### Compilation, endturn and catalogue publication
+
+The candidate catalogue is prepared during explicit compilation and published by a real owner lifecycle hook at the corresponding successful `endturn` boundary. `endturn` does not return: calling publication after it does not implement this contract. By itself it neither compiles, discovers edits nor refreshes the catalogue.
+
+Compilation facts describe the graph/representation actually consumed by that compilation. Interpreted build tests can have normal effects; later `toLmx` output must not be mislabeled as earlier lowering input. Compilation input and an optional post-test current observation are distinct named bases.
+
+An SQL transaction atomically publishes only the SQL projection. It does not combine graph stores, native installation, mail, files and network effects into one transaction. Integration reports prepared, pending, published or failed/recovery outcomes according to the actual stage. Database failure does not imply runtime rollback or a refreshed catalogue. The minimal profile keeps one active projection per owner; history is optional.
+
+After an interpreted edit without compilation, the old catalogue still describes its stated compilation. It cannot prove that a new live object is absent. Current LMX remains available through `toLmx` on the actual Structure or L3Thread. No write watcher, automatic recompilation or dependency on disk source is introduced to make the index look current.
+
+<a id="edit-api-mcp"></a>
+### MCP compatibility
+
+“Superset” means standard MCP plus native LMX and local Message paths; MCP retains its standard JSON-RPC wire. The [versioned design](../runtime_server/docs/MCP_COMPATIBILITY.ru.md) maps discovery, tools, resources, templates, prompts, completion, additional input, notifications and optional tasks to the same service layer. An advertised capability requires real implementation.
+
+The current [MCP 2026-07-28 specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28) uses stateless requests, `server/discover`, per-request protocol/capability metadata and defined result patterns. A separately selected 2025-11-25 binding retains its own initialization/session rules. These profiles do not mix envelopes, cancellation rules or task shapes. Resource URIs select exposed resources and do not become global graph identities. The runtime's outbound model API remains a separate typed dependency.
 
